.Dd November 23, 2026
.Dt NVFATBIN 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvfatbin ,
.Nm fatbinary
.Nd collect CUDA device images into one cuda-native fatbin container
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl 64
.Fl -create
.Ar file
.Fl -image3 Ns = Ns Cm kind= Ns Ar kind Ns Cm ,sm= Ns Ar number Ns Cm ,file= Ns Ar input
.Op Ar image ...
.Pp
.Nm
.Fl -version
.Op Fl -json
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
and its installed alias
.Nm fatbinary
are the same program.
The usage line and every diagnostic name the program the way it was invoked:
the basename of
.Pa argv[0] ,
so
.Nm fatbinary
says
.Ql fatbinary .
The JSON and the trace records keep the canonical name
.Ql nvfatbin .
It accepts the option subset that clang 23 emits when it collects CUDA device
images.
At least one
.Fl -image3
is required.
.Pp
The program writes one container that holds every image in argument order.
The container is LLVM's
.Em OffloadBinary
format, version 2; its fields, its string keys and the checks a reader must
do are described in
.Xr nvfatbin 5 .
No NVIDIA fatbinary or cubin format is read, parsed or imitated.
.Pp
The container is at most 16 MiB and holds at most 4096 members.
A container that would be larger is refused, because the runtime stub
refuses one that is, and no output is left at the requested path.
.Pp
A file that cannot be read says why: it is not there, the permission is
refused, the input is a directory, the input is empty, the read failed, or
the host is out of memory.
.Pp
Each image is one of
.Cm elf
or
.Cm ptx ,
and each SM value is a decimal number with an optional
.Cm a
or
.Cm f
suffix, for example
.Cm 120
or
.Cm 121a .
An
.Cm elf
image is a cubin: it must start with the cuda-native cubin magic
.Li CUDA-NATIVE-STUB-CUBIN 1 ,
and any other input is refused, so no foreign binary is ever bundled.
A
.Cm ptx
image is kept byte for byte.
.Pp
Every member records the triple
.Pa nvptx64-nvidia-cuda ,
its architecture, its format and the SHA-256 of its image.
.Pp
The output is built privately in its destination directory and renamed into
place only after every image has been read, so a failed image read leaves no
output at the requested path.
A trace that cannot be written is a different case: the container is already
in place, and the run ends with a diagnostic and exit status 1.
.Pp
.Fl 64
and
.Fl g
are accepted for clang compatibility.
.Fl -create
also accepts an equals sign before its value.
.Fl h
and
.Fl -help
print usage.
Every unsupported option, malformed image specification or extra argument is
refused instead of being ignored.
.Pp
.Fl -version
prints
.Ql NAME (cuda-native) VERSION ,
where
.Ql NAME
is the name the program was invoked as.
With
.Fl -json
it prints one JSON object containing
.Dv tool ,
.Dv version ,
.Dv version_major ,
.Dv version_minor ,
.Dv version_patch ,
.Dv stub
and
.Dv track .
The version string and its three integer parts come from
.Dv CUDA_NATIVE_VERSION
in
.Pa sdk/include/cuda.h
when
.Pa mksdk.sh
installs the tool.
The object validates against
.Pa share/cuda-native/schema/cuda-native-tool-1.schema.json .
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width CUDA_NATIVE_TRACE
.It Ev CUDA_NATIVE_TRACE
When nonempty, append one JSON record after a successful container write.
The record has
.Dv tool ,
.Dv schema ,
.Dv output ,
.Dv images
and
.Dv stub
fields.
The image array records each kind, numeric SM value, architecture and input
path in order.
The architecture is the name the container records for the image, with its
suffix: sm_120 , sm_121a .
The field
.Dv arch
is not required by the schema, so a record without it is still valid.
The record is validated by
.Pa share/cuda-native/schema/nvfatbin-trace-1.schema.json
in the SDK tree.
Arguments containing control bytes or malformed UTF-8 are refused before
writing output or a trace.
Valid UTF-8 paths are accepted, and JSON metacharacters are escaped, keeping
every emitted string parseable as UTF-8 JSON.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
In detail, exit status 0 means that the container was written, 2 reports
usage or an unsupported argument, and 1 reports an input, output or trace
error.
A failed image read leaves no output at the requested path.
A failed trace write leaves the container at the requested path.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Collect one cubin and its PTX source:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fatbinary -64 --create kernel.fatbin \e
    --image3=kind=elf,sm=121,file=kernel.cubin \e
    --image3=kind=ptx,sm=121,file=kernel.ptx
.Ed
.Pp
List what the container holds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cuda-objdump kernel.fatbin
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cuda-objdump 1 ,
.Xr nvptxas 1 ,
.Xr libcudart-stub 3 ,
.Xr nvfatbin 5 ,
.Xr cuda-native-toolchain 7
.Sh CAVEATS
The container is a cuda-native format built on LLVM's layout.
It is not an NVIDIA fatbinary and no NVIDIA fatbinary tool reads it.
It neither assembles PTX nor makes device code executable.
The stub runtime validates and counts the members of the container for
host-side registration, but never registers its contents with a GPU.
No NVIDIA CUDA toolkit file is used.
.Pp
The device image of a
.Fl -emit-llvm
compile is an LLVM IR file, not a cubin, and it is not bundled: only our own
cubins are, so a foreign binary is never carried in a container.
A
.Fl -emit-llvm
compile therefore fails with
.Ql not a cuda-native cubin .
Use
.Fl -c
or
.Fl -o
to build an object or an executable instead.
